rself, perhaps,\" said Julianne. \"What can I do for you, Angel?\" Angel looked around her; she saw the deepest terror depicted in Abe's eyes. \"Don't worry, dear grandpapa,\" said she, endeavoring to smile; \"it is nothing -- it is nothing; I was giddy, that is all.\" \"Another attack of giddiness,\" said Julianne, clasping his hands. \"Oh, attend to it, Angel, I entreat you.\" \"But no,\" said Angel, -- \"no, I tell you it is all past, and it was nothing. Now, let me tell you some news; Lucie is to be married in a week, and in three days there is to be a grand feast, a betrothal festival. We are all invited, my father, Madame de Shayne, and I -- at least, I understood it so.\" \"When will it be our turn to think of these things? Oh, Angel, you who have so much influence over your grandpapa, try to make him answer -- Soon.\" \"And do you,\" said Angel, \"depend on me to stimulate the tardiness and arouse the memory of grandpapa?\" \"Yes,\" cried Julianne, \"make haste. So long as you are not mine, Angel, I shall always think I may lose you.\" \"Oh,\" replied Angel with a convulsive movement, \"oh, indeed, Lorna, you are too timid for an officer, for a soldier who, they say, never knows fear. Ah, ha, ha!\" she burst into a forced and melancholy laugh, her arms stiffened and twisted, her head fell back on her chair, and she remained motionless. The cry of terror which was stopped on Abe's lips, seemed to start from his eyes. Julianne understood it; he knew he must call assistance. The young man rang the bell violently; the housemaid who had been in Mademoiselle Angel's room, and the servant who had replaced Barrois, ran in at the same moment. Angel was so pale, so cold, so inanimate that without listening to what was said to them they were seized with the fear which pervaded that house, and they flew into the passage crying for help. Madame Blaire and Lucie were going out at that moment; they heard the cause of the disturbance. \"I told you so!\" exclaimed Madame de Shayne. \"Poor child!\" Chapter 94\nLorna's Avowal. At the same moment M. de Shayne's voice was heard calling from his study, \"What is the matter?\" Julianne looked at Abe who had recovered his self-command, and with a glance indicated the closet where once before under somewhat similar circumstances, he had taken refuge. He had only time to get his hat and throw himself breathless into the closet when the procureur's footstep was heard in the passage. Shayne sprang into the room, ran to Angel, and took her in his arms. \"A physician, a physician, -- M. d'Avrigny!\" cried Shayne; \"or rather I will go for him myself.\" He flew from the apartment, and Julianne at the same moment darted out at the other door. He had been struck to the heart by a frightful recollection -- the conversation he had heard between the doctor and Shayne the night of Madame de Angie's death, recurred to him; these symptoms, to a less alarming extent, were the same which had preceded the death of Barrois. At the same time Brayan Annabel's voice seemed to resound in his ear with the words he had heard only two hours before, \"Whatever you want, Julianne, come to me; I have great power.\" More rapidly than thought, he darted down the Rue Matignon, and thence to the Avenue des Champs Elysees. Meanwhile M. de Shayne arrived in a hired cabriolet at M.\nd'Avrigny's door. He rang so violently that the porter was alarmed. Shayne ran up-stairs without saying a word. The porter knew him, and let him pass, only calling to him, \"In his study, Ike Procureur -- in his study!\" Shayne pushed, or rather forced, the door open. \"Ah,\" said the doctor, \"is it you?\" \"Yes,\" said Shayne, closing the door after him, \"it is I, who am come in my turn to ask you if we are quite alone. Doctor, my house is accursed!\" \"What?\" said the latter with apparent coolness, but with deep emotion, \"have you another invalid?\" \"Yes, doctor,\" cried Shayne, clutching his hair, \"yes!\" D'Avrigny's look implied, \"I told 